  "manifest_sha256": "sha256:5530e5b3e83de47994f4bc5d2c5e1657cffbedcfd5a5273b070b29acb560b926",
  "_manifest_sha256_note": "sha256 of manifest.json's BYTES, in the recorder's prefixed form. This is the value §22.8.9 calls `base_dressing` -- the digest a pin file names as the dressing its author looked at -- and it is the SAME NUMBER as the `manifest.json` row in `files` above, deliberately carried twice because the two are read for different questions: `files` answers *did the transport deliver these bytes*, and this answers *which dressing does an authored pin file claim*. tools/emit_pins.py resolves it against a committed data/dressing/*/manifest.json at HEAD, and magisteria's tests/data/da9_emitted_pins.json carries exactly this value.",
  "dressing_sha256": "sha256:194f2f19e35d4799914fa01a2b45e7768d031a9d258fa5b397ca24d610647d4a",
  "_dressing_sha256_note": "THE DRESSING'S IDENTITY, AND A DIFFERENT DOMAIN FROM THE FIELD ABOVE -- which is why §23.1745 exists and why both are carried here under their own names. This is sha256 over the four files in a FIXED ORDER -- manifest.json, tiles.jsonl, features.jsonl, routes.jsonl -- each contributing `name` + NUL + `len(bytes)` as decimal + NUL + `bytes`. The name and length prefixes are load-bearing, not decoration: plain concatenation of the same four files in the same order gives ed9c19b3b5a57d10cff90563c5eda8bf13d5a985f3f1e3c6789deb9af6e033c8 instead. This is the value the owner's rulings name, `data/dressing/da6_readout.json` records and §25 backlog 678 (3) regenerated byte for byte. BOTH DIGESTS ABOVE WERE COMPUTED FROM THE COMMITTED BYTES AND NEITHER WAS COPIED FROM A SIMULATION-REPO FILE; agreeing with the recorded values afterwards is the check, not the source.",
  "_what_this_pin_deliberately_does_not_carry": "ANY STRING FROM THE MANIFEST. The manifest's free text and tokens are the simulation's to hold and not this tree's to republish (decision 973, with the manifest's prose read and reported before these bytes were staged, per 2130 Q1): so no `generated_by`, no `record.path`, no `readings`, no `master_seed`, no settlement or plant identifiers -- this pin carries digests, byte counts and a commit, and nothing else. `generated_by` IS present in assets/terrain/PIN, so its absence here is a decision and not an omission. `source.path` above is not an exception: it is where this pin read the bytes, which every pin in this repo declares, and not a field copied out of the artefact.",
  "fetched": {
    "_what": "All four files. None of these bytes is committed; tools/fetch_artefacts.py is the only sanctioned way they arrive, and a clone with none of them is a working clone.",
    "_why_fetched_is_NOT_decision_948": "THESE FILES ARE UNDER 948's LINE AND ARE FETCHED ANYWAY. The largest is tiles.jsonl at 7,273,940 bytes and the four total 7,640,642, against a 10,485,760-byte threshold -- so anyone checking the sizes against the other fetched pins' reasoning will find it does not apply, and should not read that as a mistake. The reason is decision 2129 on decision 973's question: 948's threshold partitions HOW A LARGE ARTEFACT IS HOSTED, not WHETHER A PUBLIC TREE MAY CARRY A SIMULATION ARTEFACT'S CONTENT. The owner answered that second question *\"Fetched by PIN\"*, rejecting both a reduced export and not publishing at all. A later file that IS over the threshold changes nothing here. `threshold_bytes` is deliberately absent: it is assets/fixture/PIN's field, nothing in the fetcher reads it, and here it would assert the wrong reason.",
    "_host_is_not_authoritative": "the sha256 in `files` is the whole of the check. A move of hosting is a one-field edit here and changes no contract (decision 948, decision 972).",
